WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:24.100
Welcome to This Explainer. Today, we're diving into a comprehensive, test-first approach to building user interfaces. We're going to break down exactly how to systematically define, build, and test a product interface, but strictly from a true product manager's perspective. You know, the goal here isn't just to make things look pretty, it's to guarantee they actually work for the user. So let's unpack this together.

2
00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:29.290
Picture this. A user looks at your brand new screen for exactly five seconds.

3
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.410
Would they instantly know what to do? Or would they be completely lost?

4
00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:35.850
It's a pretty harsh reality check, right?

5
00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:43.390
But honestly, it's the exact mindset we need to adopt before we ever start sketching out wireframes or writing a single line of code.

6
00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:48.140
Here's our roadmap for working backwards from a rigorous usability test.

7
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:53.840
We're going to cover the five-second test and SUS score, the seven core interface principles.

8
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.760
defining your design spec, mapping metrics and edge cases, and finally the most common testing

9
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:05.720
mistakes to avoid. All right, let's jump right into section one, the ultimate trial by fire for

10
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:11.560
any interface, which is the five second test and the system usability scale. So here's how you

11
00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:17.160
actually run a quick, incredibly effective five second test. You just flash a screen to a user for

12
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:22.360
five seconds, take it away and ask them three specific questions. What is the screen? Who is

13
00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:26.360
it for and what is the main action you can take here. If the user can't answer

14
00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:29.860
those immediately, well your information architecture needs work before you

15
00:01:29.860 --> 00:01:35.080
even think about visual design. Now take a look at this number 68. This represents

16
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:39.980
the industry average for the system usability scale or SUS score. The SUS

17
00:01:39.980 --> 00:01:44.140
is essentially a classic 10-question survey that measures overall usability on

18
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:49.300
a scale from 0 to 100. So 68 is the baseline. It's okay but it's really just

19
00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:54.570
average. And let's be real, we are not trying to build average products here. To see what we should

20
00:01:54.570 --> 00:02:00.750
be aiming for, you can use this SUS questionnaire to constantly iterate your designs, always aiming

21
00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:07.950
for that excellent A grade of 80+. On the flip side, any score under 50, that is a massive red

22
00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:13.510
flag. It literally demands an immediate, significant redesign because it means users are fundamentally

23
00:02:13.510 --> 00:02:19.290
struggling to use your platform. So how do we push our designs to hit that coveted 80-plus score?

24
00:02:19.770 --> 00:02:23.030
Well, that brings us to section two, the 7 p.m. interface principles.

25
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:30.450
To actually achieve that high usability score, we have to build on a really solid foundation of product principles, not just visual aesthetics.

26
00:02:31.010 --> 00:02:34.350
These are the 7 p.m. interface principles you absolutely must apply.

27
00:02:34.850 --> 00:02:39.050
For example, your interface has to be goal-oriented, meaning it minimizes distractions.

28
00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:44.970
If a user searches for something and gets hit with five ads before the actual results, yeah, that is not goal-oriented.

29
00:02:45.570 --> 00:02:49.250
It also needs to be forgiving, giving users an undo option when they mess up.

30
00:02:49.330 --> 00:02:53.890
Kind of like how Gmail gives you that 30-second window to unsend a panicked email?

31
00:02:53.890 --> 00:02:58.450
It must be predictable, so a save button always acts like a save button, and consistent so

32
00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:03.110
you aren't calling something a project on screen one and a task on screen two.

33
00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:07.830
And all of that brilliantly illustrates why product managers must carefully define the architecture

34
00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:13.170
before a designer even picks up a digital pen, which leads us right into section three,

35
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:14.990
defining the design specification.

36
00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:18.710
A crucial distinction in the product life cycle is this division of ownership.

37
00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:22.510
The PM owns the information architecture and the user goals.

38
00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:26.970
Basically, they decide why the screens exist and what they need to accomplish.

39
00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:31.670
The designer, on the other hand, owns the visual execution, the how.

40
00:03:32.190 --> 00:03:36.210
If the PM doesn't clearly define that why and what in a detailed spec first,

41
00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:38.470
the designer is pretty much flying blind.

42
00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.970
This builds directly into the rule of five for screen states.

43
00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:51.670
See, outlining a design specification means you have to document all five of these states, rather than just designing the perfect, ideal scenario.

44
00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:57.650
You've got to specify the empty state, like what prompts a user to act when no data exists yet.

45
00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:04.590
You need the loading state, the partial state, and absolutely crucially, the error state for when things inevitably go wrong.

46
00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:07.530
Honestly, forgetting these is a super common mistake.

47
00:04:08.360 --> 00:04:12.640
Once you've mapped out those states, you need to know exactly how to validate if the screen is

48
00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:17.140
functioning as intended. This brings us to Section 4, Success Metrics and Edge Cases.

49
00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:22.720
So the key here is defining crystal clear success metrics using the Given When Then formula.

50
00:04:23.180 --> 00:04:27.960
This formula basically acts as an unbreakable contract for when a screen is fully complete

51
00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:33.860
and ready to ship. So given the user is on the signup page, when they type an invalid email,

52
00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:38.140
then a specific error message appears under the field. It's simple, but it

53
00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:41.260
establishes a highly measurable benchmark for your engineering and QA

54
00:04:41.260 --> 00:04:46.060
teams. Applying that logic helps map out exactly what we expect the system to do.

55
00:04:46.060 --> 00:04:50.300
Walking through a micro scenario, like adding a team member, shows how you

56
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:55.020
must document unexpected user reactions before development even starts. If it's

57
00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.460
a first-time user, prompt them nicely. If they try to add an email that's

58
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.620
already in the team, the system needs to explicitly state, user already in team,

59
00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.740
Or, if the server times out, show a connection failure.

60
00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:09.760
Documenting this stuff now prevents massive, massive headaches later on.

61
00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:10.800
But here's the catch.

62
00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.800
Even with a flawless design specification and well-mapped edge cases,

63
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.600
your validation can totally fail if your testing methodology is flawed.

64
00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:21.960
Let's look at Section 5, Avoiding Common Testing Mistakes.

65
00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:25.800
There are some sharp contrasts in how you should approach testing.

66
00:05:26.440 --> 00:05:31.060
Testing with polite family members or verbally leading the user completely ruins your data.

67
00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:35.180
I mean, let's be honest, your mom is always going to say your app looks nice, right?

68
00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:38.980
You absolutely must test strictly with real target users.

69
00:05:39.680 --> 00:05:43.600
Also, if you find yourself having to explain the product before the test even starts,

70
00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.640
yeah, the interface has already failed.

71
00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:47.280
You have to let them struggle.

72
00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.480
Their struggle is exactly where your core learning happens.

73
00:05:51.710 --> 00:05:56.470
Amazingly, another application of the Rule of Five applies to usability testing.

74
00:05:57.110 --> 00:06:03.290
Testing with just five users from your target persona is actually enough to uncover 80% of your usability problems.

75
00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.790
You really don't need a massive, super expensive statistical study.

76
00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:09.010
You're looking for qualitative patterns here.

77
00:06:09.350 --> 00:06:12.090
If three out of five users get stuck on the exact same button,

78
00:06:12.430 --> 00:06:16.130
well, you've got a proven, fundamental design flaw that needs fixing right away.

79
00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:20.430
And this perfectly aligns with what product expert Marty Kagan points out.

80
00:06:20.630 --> 00:06:25.090
Testing early is an incredibly cheap way to prevent burning months of development time

81
00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:27.590
on an interface that users just won't understand.

82
00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:30.890
Every single screen, before it ever goes to development,

83
00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:33.410
should pass through a quick usability test.

84
00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:36.610
So keeping all these principles in mind,

85
00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:38.210
from mapping out empty states

86
00:06:38.210 --> 00:06:40.290
to running that harsh five-second test,

87
00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.130
take a really hard look at your current interface.

88
00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:45.450
If you handed it to a stranger right now

89
00:06:45.450 --> 00:06:48.310
without saying a single word, how would it actually score?

90
00:06:48.670 --> 00:06:50.490
Would it hit that excellent 80 plus

91
00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.570
or would it leave them completely stuck?

92
00:06:53.110 --> 00:06:54.370
Take this test-first approach

93
00:06:54.370 --> 00:06:56.390
to your own specs and find out.

